# The line endings of the files the workflows run.
#
# A shell script with a carriage return at the end of its lines does not
# run on Linux or macOS: the interpreter reads the return as part of
# the command. Git is told to keep these files as they are written, so
# a script that works on the machine that wrote it works on the runner
# that runs it.
#
# The same applies to the workflow files, the manifests and the
# documentation. Git normalises them to line feeds in the repository and
# puts them back the way the platform expects in the working tree, so
# the files look the same on every machine and are stored the same way in
# the history.

*.sh text eol=lf

# The hooks have no extension, so the rule above does not reach them. A
# hook with a carriage return at the end of its lines fails on the first
# line, and it fails on the machine of the person who pushes rather than
# on the runner.
.husky/* text eol=lf

*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
